Q: How to enable GitLab Container Registry and push docker image in it A: <p>I have my own nginx reverse proxy for gitlab. This configuration works fine for me:</p>
<pre class="lang-rb prettyprint-override"><code>        # registry
        registry['enable'] = true
        gitlab_rails['registry_enabled'] = true
        registry_external_url 'https://registry.mycompany.com'
        gitlab_rails['registry_host'] = &quot;registry.mycompany.com&quot;
        registry['registry_http_addr'] = &quot;0.0.0.0:5000&quot;
        registry_nginx['enable'] = false
</code></pre>
<p>Here is my nginx reverse proxy for registry:</p>
<pre class="lang-ini prettyprint-override"><code>server {
   listen 80;
   listen [::]:80;  
   server_name registry.mycompany.com;
   return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
}

server {
  listen 443 ssl http2;
  listen [::]:443 ssl http2;

  server_name registry.mycompany.com;

  server_tokens             off;
  root                      /dev/null;

  ssl_certificate /certs/fullchain.pem;
  ssl_certificate_key /certs/privkey.pem;

  # Improve HTTPS performance with session resumption
  ssl_session_cache shared:SSL:10m;
  ssl_session_timeout 10m;

  # Enable server-side protection against BEAST attacks
  ssl_protocols TLSv1.2;
  ssl_prefer_server_ciphers on;
  ssl_ciphers &quot;ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-ECDSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305:ECDHE-RSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305:ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-SHA384:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-SHA384&quot;;

  # Aditional Security Headers
  # ref: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Security/HTTP_Strict_Transport_Security
  add_header Strict-Transport-Security &quot;max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains&quot;;

  # ref: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/X-Frame-Options
  add_header X-Frame-Options DENY always;

  # ref: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/X-Content-Type-Options
  add_header X-Content-Type-Options nosniff always;

  # ref: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/X-XSS-Protection
  add_header X-Xss-Protection &quot;1; mode=block&quot; always;

  # Enable OCSP stapling 
  # ref. http://blog.mozilla.org/security/2013/07/29/ocsp-stapling-in-firefox
  ssl_stapling on;
  ssl_stapling_verify on;
  ssl_trusted_certificate /certs/fullchain.pem;

  # display gitlab errors
  proxy_intercept_errors off;

  # max size for bucket upload
  client_max_body_size 300M;

  location / {

     ## If you use https make sure you disable gzip compression
     ## to be safe against BREACH attack.
     gzip                    off;

     proxy_pass           http://127.0.0.1:5000;
     proxy_set_header     X-Real-IP       $remote_addr;
     proxy_set_header     Host            $http_host;
     proxy_set_header     Upgrade         $http_upgrade;
     proxy_set_header     Connection      &quot;Upgrade&quot;;
     proxy_set_header     X-Forwarded-For    $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
     proxy_set_header     X-Forwarded-Proto  $scheme;
     proxy_connect_timeout        10;
     proxy_http_version           1.1;
     proxy_ssl_server_name        on;
     proxy_ignore_client_abort    on;
     fastcgi_ignore_client_abort  on;
     proxy_buffering              off;
     proxy_intercept_errors       on;
     proxy_buffers                16       16k;
     proxy_buffer_size            16k;
  }

}
</code></pre>

Q: How to get list of statements for a given Wikidata ID? A: <p>The information you want is in your query, except it's hard to decode. For example, this:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>&quot;P246&quot;: [
          {
            &quot;id&quot;: &quot;q568$E47B8CE7-C91D-484A-9DA4-6153F132997D&quot;,
            &quot;mainsnak&quot;: {
              &quot;snaktype&quot;: &quot;value&quot;,
              &quot;property&quot;: &quot;P246&quot;,
              &quot;datatype&quot;: &quot;string&quot;,
              &quot;datavalue&quot;: {
                &quot;value&quot;: &quot;Li&quot;,
                &quot;type&quot;: &quot;string&quot;
              }
            },
            &quot;type&quot;: &quot;statement&quot;,
            &quot;rank&quot;: &quot;normal&quot;,
            &quot;references&quot;: …
          }
        ]
</code></pre>
<p>means that the “element symbol” (<a href="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Property:P246" rel="nofollow noreferrer">property P246</a>) is “Li”. So, you will need to read all the properties from your query and then find out the name for each of the properties you found.</p>
<p>To get just the statements, you could also use <code>action=wbgetclaims</code>, but it's in the same format as above.</p>

Q: How I can remove all odds bits in C? A: <p>type-generic solution up to 128 bits based on <a href="/a/45695465/2461638">chux' answer</a>:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;limits.h&gt;

// set n least significant bits
#define SetLSBs(type, n) (~(type)0 &gt;&gt; (sizeof(type) * CHAR_BIT - (n)))

#define RemoveOddBits_Implementation(type, x) \
    for (unsigned shift = 1; shift &lt;= sizeof(type) * 2; shift *= 2) { \
        const type \
            mask1 = ~(type)0 / SetLSBs(type, shift * 4) * SetLSBs(type, shift), \
            mask2 = mask1 &lt;&lt; (shift * 2); \
        x = (x &amp; mask1) | ((x &amp; mask2) &gt;&gt; shift); \
    } \
    return x;

#define RemoveOddBits_MakeImplementation(type, suffix) \
    type RemoveOddBits_##suffix(type x) { RemoveOddBits_Implementation(type, x) }
RemoveOddBits_MakeImplementation(unsigned, u);
RemoveOddBits_MakeImplementation(unsigned long, ul);
RemoveOddBits_MakeImplementation(unsigned long long, ull);
RemoveOddBits_MakeImplementation(unsigned __int128, u128);

#define RemoveOddBits(x) _Generic(x, \
    unsigned: RemoveOddBits_u, \
    unsigned long: RemoveOddBits_ul, \
    unsigned long long: RemoveOddBits_ull, \
    unsigned __int128: RemoveOddBits_u128)
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/xb4nG6r49" rel="nofollow noreferrer">GCC</a> and <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/YqqbPrfW6" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Clang</a> unroll the loop inside <code>RemoveOddBits_Implementation</code> and correctly replace the shift and mask values with literals.</p>

Q: Switch seperate argv character in seperate function A: <p>First, your <code>main()</code> function is declared wrong. The <code>argc</code> parameter needs to be <code>int</code> instead of <code>int*</code>.</p>
<p>Second, <code>while (++argv)</code> is an infinite loop, so you end up going out of bounds of the <code>argv</code> array.  Your loop condition is not dereferencing <code>argv</code> to test whether it's pointing at a valid string before the loop body tries to process it.</p>
<p>Besides, this is simply not the correct way to loop through <code>argv</code> anyway. <code>argv</code> is not a null-terminated array, as you are assuming, so you can't use a <code>while</code> loop in this manner to look for a null pointer to terminate the loop, because there is no guarantee that such a null pointer even exists.</p>
<p><code>argc</code> tells you exactly how many strings are in the <code>argv</code> array, so you need to pass <code>argc</code> as well to your <code>switch_argument()</code> function, and then use a <code>for</code> loop instead of a <code>while</code> loop, eg:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>void switch_argument (int argc, char const *argv[])
{
    // start at idx=1 to skip the program name in argv[0]
    for (int idx = 1; idx &lt; argc; ++idx)
    {
        if (argv[idx][0] == '-')
        {
            switch (argv[idx][1])
            {
                case 'p':
                    printf(&quot;Pack.\n&quot;);
                    break;
                case 'u':
                    printf(&quot;Unpack.\n&quot;);
                    break;
                default:
                    Helper();
            }
        }
    }
}

int main(int argc, char const *argv[])
{
    // Check for 3 arguments
    if (argc &gt;= 3)
    {
        switch_argument(argc, argv);
    }
    else
    {
        printf(&quot;Too few arguments.\n&quot;);
        Helper();
    }

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Does RavenDB (DabDB) support CROSS JOIN with the Northwind dataset? A: <p>If you're finding out how many products are ordered as per product, then you can use a map-reduce</p>
<p>In map</p>
<pre><code>from o in docs.Orders
from l in o.Lines
let p = LoadDocument(l.Product,&quot;Products&quot;)
select new{
    ProductName = p.Name,
    Count = 1
} 
</code></pre>
<p>In Reduce</p>
<pre><code>from r in results
group r by r.ProductName into g 
select new {
    ProductName = g.Key,
    Count = g.Sum(x =&gt; x.Count) 
}
</code></pre>
<p>In RQL</p>
<pre><code>from index &quot;prod_cunt&quot; 
where ProductName == &quot;Tofu&quot;
</code></pre>

Q: C in Eclipse: scanf and buffers A: <p>First of all, the line</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>char string[] = &quot;&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>is equivalent to</p>
<pre><code>char string[1] = &quot;&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>which is not a sufficient array size to store a number. It is only sufficient for storing an empty string, which consists of nothing else than the terminating null character. I suggest that you increase the size of the array to a much higher number (maybe several hundred characters).</p>
<p>Using <code>scanf</code> for line-based user input is generally not recommended, because <code>scanf</code> was not designed for that. The <code>%s</code> conversion format specification will discard all leading <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/string/byte/isspace" rel="nofollow noreferrer">whitespace characters</a>, which means that it won't distinguish between spaces and newline characters. This is not what you want.</p>
<p>A better solution is to use the function <code>fgets</code> to always read an entire line of input, and then to use the function <code>strtol</code> to convert this input to an integer. Here is an example:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;
#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;
#include &lt;limits.h&gt;
#include &lt;errno.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;

int sum_integers( void );
bool get_int_from_user( int *val, bool *trailing_input_ignored );

int main( void )
{
    printf( &quot;The sum is: %d\n&quot;, sum_integers() );
}

int sum_integers( void )
{
    int total = 0;
    int num;
    bool trailing_input_ignored;

    printf( &quot;Enter integers, one per line:\n&quot; );
    fflush( stdout );

    while ( get_int_from_user( &amp;num, &amp;trailing_input_ignored ) )
    {
        total += num;

        if ( trailing_input_ignored )
        {
            printf( &quot;Warning: Ignoring trailing input!\n&quot; );
            fflush( stdout );
        }
    }

    return total;
}

// This function will read an entire line of input from the user, and
// attempt to convert the first non-whitespace characters it finds to
// an integer.
//
// On success, it will return true and write the value of this integer
// to the object pointed to by the parameter &quot;val&quot;. On failure, it will
// return false.
//
// It will also set the object referenced by the &quot;trailing_input_ignored&quot;
// parameter to true or false depending on whether trailing
// non-whitespace input was ignored or not.
//
bool get_int_from_user( int *val, bool *trailing_input_ignored )
{
    char buffer[1024], *p;
    long num;

    // get one line of input from standard input
    if ( fgets( buffer, sizeof buffer, stdin ) == NULL )
    {
        fprintf( stderr, &quot;Unrecoverable input error!\n&quot; );
        exit( EXIT_FAILURE );
    }

    // make sure that entire line was read in (i.e. that
    // the buffer was not too small)
    if ( strchr( buffer, '\n' ) == NULL &amp;&amp; !feof( stdin ) )
    {
        fprintf( stderr, &quot;Input line was too long!\n&quot; );
        exit( EXIT_FAILURE );
    }

    // attempt to convert string to number
    errno = 0;
    num = strtol( buffer, &amp;p, 10 );
    if ( p == buffer )
    {
        // conversion failed, so input must be not a number
        *val = 0;
        *trailing_input_ignored = false;
        return false;
    }

    // make sure that number is representable as an &quot;int&quot;
    if ( errno == ERANGE || num &lt; INT_MIN || num &gt; INT_MAX )
    {
        fprintf( stderr, &quot;Number out of range error!\n&quot; );
        exit( EXIT_FAILURE );
    }

    // skip all whitespace characters
    while ( isspace( (unsigned char)*p ) )
    {
        p++;
    }

    // conversion was successful, so hand back appropriate values
    // to caller function
    *val = num;
    *trailing_input_ignored = *p != '\0';
    return true;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The function <code>get_int_from_user</code> is a modified version of the function of the same name taken from <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/73915292/12149471">this answer of mine to another question</a>. I suggest that you read that answer for details on how that function works.</p>
<p>This program has the following behavior:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Enter integers, one per line:
2
3 5
Warning: Ignoring trailing input!
10
quit
The sum is: 15
</code></pre>
